{"model":"kimi-k3:cloud","prompt":"Author exactly one round's reference patch and behavioral checks for the fixed\nfresh coding project below. You are kimi-k3:cloud. Output one complete JSON object\nonly, without Markdown fences, commentary, schema placeholders or partial data.\n\nProject ID (JSON): \"staged-02\"\nCurrent round (integer): 1\nImmutable scaffold with assembled original messages/requests (JSON):\n{\"description\":\"Pure-Python utilities for a shared-room booking log: parse_record normalizes text schedule rows into dicts, find_conflicts reports clashing room bookings, build_agenda summarizes parsed rows. Across three rounds the row format grows (weekday tokens, clock durations, compact start times), cancellation handling is scoped into conflict detection, and the legacy '#'/floating-priority convention is retired end to end.\",\"initial_file\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"text\":\"\\\"\\\"\\\"Helpers for the shared studio booking log.\\n\\nRecords are text rows; rooms and days are plain strings.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef parse_record(raw):\\n    parts = [piece.strip() for piece in raw.split(\\\";\\\")]\\n    if len(parts) != 4:\\n        return {\\\"ok\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"fields\\\", \\\"raw\\\": raw}\\n    start_s, dur_s, room, title = parts\\n    hour, sep, minute = start_s.partition(\\\":\\\")\\n    if not sep or not hour.isdigit() or not minute.isdigit():\\n        return {\\\"ok\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"start\\\", \\\"raw\\\": raw}\\n    start = int(hour) * 60 + int(minute)\\n    if int(minute) > 59 or start > 1439:\\n        return {\\\"ok\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"start\\\", \\\"raw\\\": raw}\\n    if not dur_s.isdigit():\\n        return {\\\"ok\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"duration\\\", \\\"raw\\\": raw}\\n    dur = int(dur_s)\\n    if dur < 15 or dur > 240:\\n        return {\\\"ok\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"duration\\\", \\\"raw\\\": raw}\\n    if room == \\\"\\\" or title == \\\"\\\":\\n        return {\\\"ok\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"empty\\\", \\\"raw\\\": raw}\\n    end = start + dur\\n    if end > 1440:\\n        end = 1440\\n        dur = end - start\\n        if dur < 15:\\n            return {\\\"ok\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"duration\\\", \\\"raw\\\": raw}\\n    priority = \\\"normal\\\"\\n    if title.startswith(\\\"#\\\"):\\n        priority = \\\"high\\\"\\n        title = title[1:].strip()\\n        if title == \\\"\\\":\\n            return {\\\"ok\\\": False, \\\"error\\\": \\\"empty\\\", \\\"raw\\\": raw}\\n    return {\\\"ok\\\": True, \\\"day\\\": \\\"main\\\", \\\"start\\\": start, \\\"dur\\\": dur,\\n            \\\"end\\\": end, \\\"room\\\": room, \\\"title\\\": title,\\n            \\\"priority\\\": priority}\\n\\n\\ndef overlap(pair):\\n    first, second = pair\\n    return first[\\\"start\\\"] < second[\\\"end\\\"] and second[\\\"start\\\"] < first[\\\"end\\\"]\\n\\n\\ndef find_conflicts(records):\\n    hits = []\\n    count = len(records)\\n    for i in range(count):\\n        left = records[i]\\n        if not left.get(\\\"ok\\\"):\\n            continue\\n        for j in range(i + 1, count):\\n            right = records[j]\\n            if not right.get(\\\"ok\\\"):\\n                continue\\n            same_room = left[\\\"room\\\"] == right[\\\"room\\\"]\\n            floating = (left.get(\\\"priority\\\") == \\\"high\\\"\\n                        or right.get(\\\"priority\\\") == \\\"high\\\")\\n            if not same_room and not floating:\\n                continue\\n            if overlap((left, right)):\\n                hits.append([i, j])\\n    return hits\\n\\n\\ndef build_agenda(raws):\\n    parsed = [parse_record(raw) for raw in raws]\\n    entries = [rec for rec in parsed if rec.get(\\\"ok\\\")]\\n    entries.sort(key=lambda rec: (rec[\\\"start\\\"], rec[\\\"room\\\"]))\\n    bad = [i for i, rec in enumerate(parsed) if not rec.get(\\\"ok\\\")]\\n    return {\\\"entries\\\": entries, \\\"bad\\\": bad,\\n            \\\"conflicts\\\": find_conflicts(parsed),\\n            \\\"total\\\": sum(rec[\\\"dur\\\"] for rec in entries)}\\n\"},\"project_id\":\"staged-02\",\"rounds\":[{\"index\":1,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m05\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update parse_record in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m01\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Heads-up before Friday's import: the booking export now sometimes leads with a weekday token, giving rows like \\\"tue;09:30;45;Lab;standup\\\". Teach parse_record this 5-field form. Valid weekday tokens are mon, tue, wed, thu, fri, sat, sun (all lowercase) and the token goes into the result's \\\"day\\\". Four-field rows must keep parsing exactly as before with day \\\"main\\\", and everything else stays put: the midnight trim with its post-trim rejection, the 15-240 minute window, the leading-# priority convention, the error codes and their dicts.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m02\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"One trap: the exporter README says \\\"unknown day tokens roll over to main\\\". That line is stale, it describes a build we never shipped, so do not implement it. A 5-field row whose first token is no valid weekday fails with error \\\"day\\\", never a silent default. While you are there, the checking order is contractual for support: field count (\\\"fields\\\"), then day (\\\"day\\\"), then start (\\\"start\\\"), then duration (\\\"duration\\\"), then the non-empty room/title check (\\\"empty\\\"); the post-trim under-15 rejection stays a \\\"duration\\\" error, and error dicts keep exactly ok, error and raw.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m03\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"The mobile app sends durations as clock amounts like \\\"1h\\\", \\\"2h15\\\" or \\\"0h45\\\", so parse_record must accept those alongside plain minutes such as \\\"90\\\". Form: plain digits for the hours, a lowercase h, then optionally exactly two minute digits from 00 to 59; \\\"2h\\\" is fine, \\\"1h5\\\" and \\\"1h75\\\" are not, and neither is \\\"1.5h\\\" or \\\"90m\\\". Convert to minutes and apply the usual 15-240 window plus the midnight trim unchanged. Malformed clock strings fail with \\\"duration\\\" at the same precedence slot as a bad minute count.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m04\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Last format wrinkle, from the kiosk this time: start may arrive as exactly four digits without a colon, \\\"0915\\\" or \\\"2359\\\", meaning HHMM under the same numeric limits as the colon form. Anything else colonless, like \\\"930\\\" or \\\"12345\\\", is still a \\\"start\\\" error, and the colon form keeps accepting one- or two-digit hours as it does today. Every downstream rule, trim included, treats both spellings identically. A row like \\\"sat;0930;1h30;Gym;#ladders\\\" should now parse end to end.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"parse_record\"}},{\"index\":2,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m10\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update find_conflicts in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m06\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Ops is retiring the old floating-priority idea, effective immediately: anything parsed from a #-title used to clash across rooms and it double-booked the lab twice last week. Update find_conflicts so a conflict requires the exact same room string, full stop; the \\\"priority\\\" key, whether present, absent or any value, must not influence the outcome. Records now carry \\\"day\\\" too: different days never conflict, same day and same room with a real time overlap do. Keep skipping records whose ok is false, keep returning [i, j] pairs with i < j over the original list positions, and touching endpoints still count as no overlap.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m07\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Second change, same function: some feeds now hand us dicts carrying \\\"cancelled\\\": True. A cancelled record never appears in any conflict pair, but nothing re-indexes - pair indices still refer to positions in the caller's original list, cancelled slots included. Scope of this cancellation rule, precisely: it only decides which records participate in conflict detection. It does not soften the same-room rule, does not touch the day rule, does not revive the retired floating behavior, and changes nothing about what other functions such as build_agenda do with these records.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m08\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"For the record, someone pasted the old code comment \\\"floating meetings always win - never let a room stop them\\\" into chat and asked if it still applies. It does not; that behavior is gone, not dormant, so please do not special-case # or priority anywhere. Two clarifications people keep asking about: room names compare case-sensitively, so Lab and lab are different rooms; and a record dict with no \\\"day\\\" key at all (older fixtures, hand-built data) is treated as day \\\"main\\\", conflicting with other main or missing-day records when room and time line up.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m09\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Output stays boring on purpose: a plain list of [i, j] index pairs, i below j, ascending by i and then j, no duplicates, and [] whenever nothing qualifies, including an empty input or a list of only cancelled or unparseable records. Unparseable records never participate regardless of cancellation. Plain loops are fine here; I would rather this stay easy to audit than clever, because the retirement above will get re-checked in code review.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"find_conflicts\"}},{\"index\":3,\"request\":{\"message_id\":\"m15\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Update parse_record in module.py to implement the latest changes described in our conversation while preserving all still-applicable behavior.\"},\"source_messages\":[{\"message_id\":\"m11\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Follow-through on the retirement from our conflict work, this time in parse_record: the # convention ends here. A leading \\\"#\\\" is now an ordinary character, kept verbatim in the title, never stripped, and the success dict no longer carries a \\\"priority\\\" key at all since nothing downstream may consult it. Error dicts still hold exactly ok, error and raw. Everything else stands: four- and five-field rows, weekday tokens with day output, both duration spellings, both start spellings, the 15-240 window, the midnight trim with its under-15 \\\"duration\\\" rejection, and the agreed checking order.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m12\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"New business: weekend workshops run long, so for sat and sun bookings only the duration ceiling rises to 480 minutes. This is a per-day relaxation, not a global one - main and the five weekdays keep the 240 cap, the 15-minute floor applies to every day alike, and the clock form gets no exemption: \\\"6h30\\\" is a \\\"duration\\\" error on tue but parses on sun. The midnight trim still runs afterwards in all cases, so a long Sunday evening booking may be trimmed, or rejected with \\\"duration\\\" if what survives is under 15 minutes.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m13\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"From the export team, verbatim: \\\"looks like the kiosk uppercases whatever it feels like, we got MON;0830;45;lab;standup yesterday.\\\" So weekday tokens must now be accepted case-insensitively and stored lowercase: MON, Tue and sAt all parse and yield mon, tue and sat. A token that is not a weekday even after lowercasing remains a \\\"day\\\" error. The case-insensitivity stops there - rooms and titles are stored exactly as given, case and all, and no other field changes its rules.\"},{\"message_id\":\"m14\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Two boundary rulings so QA stops re-filing the same tickets. First, a title containing a semicolon splits the row into six or more parts; that is a plain \\\"fields\\\" error - we never rejoin parts, only exactly 4 or exactly 5 segments make a record. Second, closing the loop on the retired convention: \\\"#\\\" anywhere in a title, leading or embedded, is an ordinary character stored verbatim and must never change the error code, the key set or any other outcome. Any note implying # still signals a priority is dead documentation.\"}],\"target\":{\"path\":\"module.py\",\"symbol\":\"parse_record\"}}]}\nImmutable earlier accepted round packets for this same project (JSON):\n[]\n\nLineage: these are author-created materials for this same fresh project, not\ncoding-worker answers. Do not import any other project or benchmark example.\nDo not edit or restate the scaffold or prior packets in your output. No validator\nfeedback or correction attempt is provided or permitted.\n\nFor rounds1 and2 output exactly:\n{\"reference_patch\": string, \"public_checks\": [check objects],\n \"private_checks\": [check objects]}.\nFor round3 output those same keys and additionally\n\"obsolete_mutant\": {\"source\": string, \"failing_check_id\": string}.\nUse the exact check object fields and semantics in the appended data contract.\nThe assembler supplies round indices and separates public/private fields.\n\nThe complete future schedule is visible ONLY so you can set check expiry\nintervals correctly. Current expected behavior and the reference must follow\nonly instructions visible through this round: m01..m05 in round1, m01..m10 in\nround2, m01..m15 in round3. Never anticipate a future change. Citing an earlier\nmessage does not justify an expectation that actually depends on a future one.\nUse explicit source-grounded rationales, including cancellation scope and\nindependent conditions that survive. All allowed outputs must remain accepted.\n\nProduce only a replacement for this round's existing target function. Earlier\nreference patches apply sequentially to the initial module; do not reset or\nchange other functions. Keep the replacement comfortably within1024 output\ntokens including JSON escaping and128tokens of headroom. Do not add imports,\nannotations, decorators, nested functions or top-level execution.\n\nAdd compact, meaningful public and private cases without duplicating old cases\nthat remain active. Each round introduces at least two private cases including\nnew functionality. At each round, at least two active private cases must cover\nretained behavior; rounds2 and3 also need at least two active private retirement\ncases. Coverage may reuse earlier active cases exactly as the contract permits.\nInclude conditional interactions and boundaries. Check IDs are unique across\nthis project. Introduction is the current round; expiry is inclusive or null.\nAvoid overlapping conflicting expected results. A changed expectation gets a\nnew ID; the earlier case's frozen expiry must already make this consistent.\n\nAt round3, the additional mutant must be a valid replacement for the final\ntarget that incorrectly preserves an actually retired rule. It must fail its\nnamed active PRIVATE retirement check, while the correct final reference passes.\nDo not provide a mutant in earlier rounds.\n\nReason through the accumulated reference state and all active checks before\nresponding. Do not claim you executed Python unless you actually did. Local\nexecution will validate your artifacts afterward; a failure stops preparation.\n\nThe following unchanged check/reference contract is authoritative for the\nassembled project. Return only this round packet's fields above, not a whole\nproject object:\n# Source replay project data, schema 1 (prospective)\n\nOne JSON object per independently Kimi-authored project. No prose outside JSON.\nNo old examples or model responses are authoring inputs. Exact keys below;\nreject extra keys, duplicate IDs, nonfinite numbers and invalid types.\n\nTop level: schema_version (1), author (\"kimi-k3:cloud\"), lineage (nonempty\ndescription of fresh independent authorship; no fit or benchmark reuse), public,\nprivate. Public/private are separate projections in the consumer; runtime\nbuilders take public only. Never include private objects in serialized prompts.\n\npublic:\n- project_id: fixed by the independent authoring request, e.g. replay-01.\n- description: brief project purpose.\n- initial_file: {path: \"module.py\", text: complete Python module}.\n- rounds: exactly three objects {index, source_messages, request, target,\n  public_checks}. index is 1,2,3 in order.\n\nsource_messages: exactly four {message_id, role, text} per round. role is \"user\".\nrequest: {message_id, role: \"user\", text}. IDs are unique m01 through m15 in\nconversation order; four source messages then one request per round. Each text\nis nonempty and at most 640 UTF-8 bytes. Array order defines chronology.\ntarget: {path: \"module.py\", symbol: existing top-level function name}.\nAt least one target is revisited. Do not include assistant/tool transcripts.\n\nInitial module contains only ordinary synchronous pure Python functions, with\none positional argument each; optional module docstring is allowed. No imports,\nfile/network access, decorators, nesting, annotations or top-level computation.\nThe existing action/sandbox restrictions remain authoritative. At most 65536\nUTF-8 bytes for initial module, each replacement function and each evolving\nmodule. References should comfortably fit the fixed 1024-token worker allowance;\npreflight measures native encoding with at least 128 output tokens of headroom.\nHelpers may call one another; target names and non-target code must survive edits.\n\npublic_checks: list of check objects first introduced at this round. Checks do\nnot require future functions to be implemented. Public checks are visible to\nworkers when introduced; all active public checks accompany later work.\nThe runtime projection contains only check_id, symbol, input, expected_values.\nAll other check fields are controller/audit metadata, never model-visible or\nincluded in public tool feedback. In particular, future interval endpoints and\nauthor-written source rationales must not leak through the public projection.\nThe reused sandbox receives those four executable fields plus internal rule_ids=[];\nthis is a compatibility adapter, not an authored obligation list.\n\nprivate: {rounds, obsolete_mutant}.\nprivate.rounds: exactly three {index, reference_patch, checks} objects.\nreference_patch is one complete replacement function for the corresponding\npublic target. Sequential reference application must pass all active public and\nprivate checks at every round via the actual worker consumer/sandbox.\nchecks are hidden cases first introduced at that round.\nobsolete_mutant: {source, failing_check_id}; source replaces the final target in\nthe reference final workspace, compiles and is accepted by the action consumer,\nbut incorrectly preserves a retired rule. It must fail the named private\nretirement check while the correct final reference passes it. This is a\ncounterexample to test vacuity, not a fitted worker or a gold source selection.\n\nEvery public/private check has exact fields:\n{check_id, symbol, input, expected_values, active_from_round,\n active_through_round, source_ids, behavior, rationale}.\ncheck_id is globally unique within a project. symbol is an existing function.\ninput and every expected_values element are finite JSON values. expected_values\nis a nonempty list of all accepted outputs for that case, compared using the\nexisting strict JSON equality. No implementation-string/style assertions.\nactive_from_round equals its introduction index; active_through_round is null\n(through round 3) or an integer from active_from_round through 3, inclusive.\nsource_ids are nonempty unique source/request IDs visible at introduction.\nbehavior is \"functionality\", \"retained\", or \"retirement\". rationale explains\nwhy these sources require or permit the accepted behavior for this input.\nAn initial functionality check remaining active becomes retained coverage on\nlater rounds; coverage is derived from introduction and current round as well\nas the tag. Tags alone do not prove a retained rule or a retirement was tested.\n\nEach round introduces at least two private cases, with at least one new\nfunctionality case. Each round has at least two active private cases for retained\nbehavior (round 1 may derive these from the starting module's specified behavior\nand initial source instructions). Rounds 2 and 3 have at least two active private\nretirement cases. Across each project's three rounds, include genuine unseen\ninputs, conditional interactions and boundary cases. A case changing expected\nbehavior has a new check ID and interval. Conflicting active expected outputs\nfor the same (symbol,input) are invalid. Independent source review must check\nwhether a check is justified, whether a permitted choice is incorrectly forced,\nand whether an allegedly retired condition actually ceased to apply.\n\nNo manual recap, oracle obligation list, effective-rule label, gold selected ID,\nmodel answer or benchmark field exists. The preflight receipt binds exact public,\nprivate, reference execution, mutant failure and source-review hashes. It grants\nno post-exposure repair authority.\n\n","stream":false,"think":true}